** Description of the test procedure **

* General setup *
In the current directory you will find the routine run.sh that functions as a
template for the execution of a particular test. It uses th environment
variables MOL and MENU that are supposed to be set by the run.sh script that
resides in the particular test directory.  Here you have the freedom to define
several runs, e.g. depending on the test that the user has selected, look at
tests 'energy+dipole', 'linear', and 'efg', to find respectively a simple, a
more elaborate and a complicated example (the latter concerns a test for a run
that needs the DFCOEF from a previous run).

* The filtering *
Once the execution of DIRAC is filtered you can compare the new output with
the reference output that is present in the result directory. If one would
use simply the unix command diff here one would always see differences, simply
because dates and execution times will be different, because insignificant
last digits are different, or because output formatting has changed. To
focus on relevant differences we use the script crep to find the relevant
numbers that are to be checked. The idea is that you define a characteristic
text that is contained in the output just BEFORE the numbers that are to
be checked. Here you may either use a string or a regular expressions, examples
of both can be found when looking at the filter files. This defines the start
of the output section to be tested, but you also want to define the end. Here
there are two possibilities, you either know in advance how many lines you
want to test, or you don't (keep in mind that others may later introduce 
additional blank or text lines). In the first case you can simply specify the
number of lines to be test (using NLines), in the second case you have to
specify a characteristic text that closes the section. 

I hope this is clear, please look at the examples already present and see how
they take out text. Then use the one best suited for your needs and test the
working by running just crep and looking at the resulting .rep file. Iterate
until the only numbers contained in that section are the ones you want to test.

* The actual testing *
This is handled by the bdrep (text output) and drep (html output) that write
the relative differences larger than the given treshold (specified by $ERROR).
In addition a single line is written to the log if a difference is found
NB: These scripts compare ALL numbers in the extract generated by crep as they
have no way of knowing which are meaningful and which are not. YOU need to
do this by making the correct filter!

** Adding a new test, how I (LV) usually do this  **
1. Copy (but do not use cp -r, as it copies also .svn directories!) 
an existing test that has the right structure to the
next available test number. Make sure to choose a sensible name to follow the .
So : do not use 31.luuksfavoritetest, but 31.symmetry_recognition to signal
that we are testing the symmetry recognition procedure.
[Radovan: to avoid problems with the annoying .svn directories when copying
an existing test as template for a new test what you can do is
cd test
svn export http://dirac.chem.sdu.dk/svnDirac/Dirac/trunk/test/existing_test new_test
then the .svn directories are not there.]

2. Put the molecule and menu input files in the directory and modify the
run.sh command to work with these filenames. 

3. Put a correct output in the result file and modify the filter file to
take out the part that is relevant for the functionality that you want to
test. Don't test everything, e.g. the electronic Hartree-Fock energy is
already tested at several places, no need to do this in every test.
Run just crep and see whether you are satisfied with the result.

4. Go to the top directory and run testlast with the number of your new test.
Say you have added test 666.exactsolutionofDiracsequation, you then say
testlast --tests=666 and see whether it gives no error. If it does go back and
figure out what went wrong. It is a good practice to test your test, try to introduce
deliberately errors in your new code and check whether your new test catches the error -
if not, modify the test.

5. If the test works look at how long it takes to run on a single PC to see where
to add it. A rule of thumb: < 10 seconds = short, < 100 seconds = medium, otherwise = long.
Add the test then to the standard sets by modifying testlast

6. Use svn add and svn commit to store your work in the repository. Make sure to include ALL files,
don't forget the result directory that should contain the reference output and filter files.
